text:
2 Geographic vs. projected CRSs
A coordinate reference system (CRS) is the set of rules that turns a pair of numbers into a place on Earth. There are two kinds.

A geographic CRS gives positions as angles on a model of the Earth: longitude and latitude, in degrees. EPSG:4326 (WGS 84) is the most common one.
A projected CRS flattens that model onto a plane and gives positions as x and y distances, usually in meters (sometimes feet). Every projected CRS is built on top of a geographic one.

The key consequence: a degree is not a fixed distance. A degree of latitude is always about 111 km. A degree of longitude shrinks as you move toward the poles, because the meridians converge.






TipPredict



How long is one degree of longitude at Luxembourg’s latitude (about 49.6° N)? More or less than 100 km?



viewof lat = Inputs.range([0, 89], {
  value: 38.9, step: 0.1, label: "Latitude (° N)"
})







kmPerDegLat = 111.2
kmPerDegLon = 111.2 * Math.cos(lat * Math.PI / 180)

// A few reference places, to make the slider concrete
places = [
  {name: "Equator", lat: 0},
  {name: "Washington, DC", lat: 38.9},
  {name: "Luxembourg", lat: 49.6},
  {name: "Oslo", lat: 59.9},
  {name: "Svalbard", lat: 78.2}
]

{
  // Two horizontal bars, drawn to the same scale (111.2 km = full width)
  const w = Math.min(width, 640);
  const bar = (label, km, color) =&gt; html`
    &lt;div style="margin:.35rem 0"&gt;
      &lt;div style="font-size:.9rem"&gt;${label}: &lt;b&gt;${km.toFixed(1)} km&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div style="height:18px;width:${(km / kmPerDegLat) * w}px;background:${color};border-radius:3px"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;`;
  // Buttons that move the slider. Built with DOM calls because Quarto's
  // html`` template doesn't attach functions as event handlers.
  const near = places.map(p =&gt; {
    const b = document.createElement("button");
    b.textContent = p.name;
    b.className = "btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary";
    b.style.margin = ".2rem .3rem .2rem 0";
    b.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
      const input = viewof lat;
      input.value = p.lat;
      input.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", {bubbles: true}));
    });
    return b;
  });
  return html`&lt;div class="widget"&gt;
    ${bar("1° of latitude", kmPerDegLat, "#7a8ca3")}
    ${bar("1° of longitude", kmPerDegLon, "#d1495b")}
    &lt;div style="margin-top:.5rem;font-size:.9rem"&gt;Jump to: ${near}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}





























So a “square” one degree on a side is not square on the ground, and its area depends on where it is. That is why lengths, areas, and buffers measured directly in degrees are meaningless.
Now read what R tells you. Each object’s CRS is in its printout, and there’s a direct yes/no question you can ask:

# Is it longitude/latitude (degrees)?
st_is_longlat(cantons)   # sf objects

[1] TRUE

is.lonlat(elev_lux)      # terra rasters

[1] FALSE

# The ranges of the coordinates tell the same story
st_bbox(cantons)

     xmin      ymin      xmax      ymax 
 5.744140 49.447807  6.528252 50.181622 

ext(elev_lux)

SpatExtent : 49072.3397901184, 106196.374340225, 56455.2689687042, 139825.481555346 (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax)


The cantons span about 0.8 degrees east–west. The raster spans about 57,000 meters. Same country, different units, so the numbers don’t overlap. That is the whole story of section 1, and we’ll come back to it.






NoteA helpful shortcut in sf



When you ask sf for an area or distance on longitude/latitude data, it does the math on the globe and gives you meters (st_area(cantons) works). Don’t count on every function being this careful. A raster’s cell size, for example, stays in degrees.

4 Projections: flattening is a trade
You can’t flatten an orange peel without stretching or tearing it. Map projections are systematic ways of doing that stretching. They come in families, named after the flat surface the globe is projected onto:

Cylindrical: wrap a cylinder around the globe (Mercator, UTM).
Conic: set a cone on top of it (Albers, Lambert Conformal Conic). Good for mid-latitude regions that are wider than they are tall, like the US or Maryland.
Azimuthal: touch a flat plane to one point (polar maps, “distance from here” maps).

Each projection protects some properties and gives up others:

Conformal: preserves shape (angles) locally. Areas get distorted.
Equal-area: preserves area. Shapes get distorted.
Equidistant: preserves distance, but only from one point or along certain lines.
Azimuthal: preserves direction from the center point.

No flat map preserves all of these. In particular, a map can’t be both conformal and equal-area. So you choose a projection by asking what your analysis measures.






TipPredict



The red circles below are all the same size on the globe (radius 4°). On the Mercator map, how will the circles near the poles compare with the circles at the equator? What about on Equal Earth?



tc = require("topojson-client@3.1.0")
worldTopo = FileAttachment("countries-110m.json").json()
land = tc.feature(worldTopo, worldTopo.objects.land)
countries = tc.feature(worldTopo, worldTopo.objects.countries).features

// ---- Shared geometry for the projection explorer ----
// Tissot circles: identical 4-degree circles on a 30 x 15 degree grid
tissot = {
  const circles = [];
  for (let lon = -180; lon &lt; 180; lon += 30) {
    for (let lat = -75; lat &lt;= 75; lat += 15) {
      circles.push(d3.geoCircle().center([lon, lat]).radius(4)());
    }
  }
  return {type: "GeometryCollection", geometries: circles};
}

// The shortest route on the globe (a great circle) from DC to London
route = ({type: "LineString", coordinates: [[-77.04, 38.90], [-0.13, 51.51]]})

// Boxes to fit maps to. Mercator can't show the poles (they're infinitely
// far away), so its world view stops at 60° S and 84° N.
naBox = d3.geoGraticule().extent([[-128, 18], [-62, 56]]).outline()
mercBox = d3.geoGraticule().extent([[-180, -60], [180, 84]]).outline()
fitTarget = view =&gt; view === "na" ? naBox : view === "merc" ? mercBox : {type: "Sphere"}

// ---- The projections on offer, with the facts shown in the info card ----
projections = new Map([
  ["Equirectangular (plain lon/lat)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoEquirectangular(),
    view: "world", family: "Cylindrical",
    keeps: "Distance along meridians only",
    gives: "Area and shape, badly near the poles",
    epsg: "What you get when you plot EPSG:4326 data as x/y",
    use: "Quick looks. Not for measuring anything."
  }],
  ["Mercator", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoMercator(),
    view: "merc", family: "Cylindrical",
    keeps: "Shape (conformal); compass directions are straight lines",
    gives: "Area: grows rapidly toward the poles",
    epsg: "3857 (Web Mercator, used by web maps), 3395",
    use: "Navigation, web basemaps. Never for area."
  }],
  ["Equal Earth", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoEqualEarth(),
    view: "world", family: "Pseudocylindrical",
    keeps: "Area (equal-area)",
    gives: "Shape, especially near the edges",
    epsg: "8857",
    use: "World maps of anything per area: density, rates, land cover."
  }],
  ["Albers Equal Area Conic (US)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoConicEqualArea().parallels([29.5, 45.5]).rotate([96, 0]),
    view: "na", family: "Conic",
    keeps: "Area (equal-area)",
    gives: "Shape, slightly, away from the two standard parallels",
    epsg: "5070 (NAD83 / Conus Albers, used by NLCD)",
    use: "Areas and densities across the US."
  }],
  ["Lambert Conformal Conic (Maryland)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoConicConformal().parallels([38.3, 39.45]).rotate([77, 0]),
    view: "na", family: "Conic",
    keeps: "Shape (conformal)",
    gives: "Area, growing away from Maryland's latitudes",
    epsg: "26985 (NAD83 / Maryland, meters), 2248 (same, US feet)",
    use: "State Plane: Maryland agency data, local measurement."
  }],
  ["Transverse Mercator (UTM zone 18N)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoTransverseMercator().rotate([75, 0]),
    view: "na", family: "Cylindrical (turned sideways)",
    keeps: "Shape (conformal)",
    gives: "Scale grows east and west of the central meridian (75° W)",
    epsg: "26918 (NAD83 / UTM 18N), 32618 (WGS 84 / UTM 18N)",
    use: "Distances and buffers within a 6°-wide zone (DC, MD, VA)."
  }],
  ["Azimuthal Equidistant (centered on DC)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoAzimuthalEquidistant().rotate([77.04, -38.9]),
    view: "world", family: "Azimuthal",
    keeps: "Distance and direction from the center point (DC)",
    gives: "Area and shape, worst far from the center",
    epsg: "No standard code; defined around any center you choose",
    use: "\"How far is everything from here?\" maps."
  }],
  ["Orthographic (a view of the globe)", {
    make: () =&gt; d3.geoOrthographic().rotate([77.04, -38.9]),
    view: "world", family: "Azimuthal (perspective)",
    keeps: "Looks like the globe from space",
    gives: "Shows only half the Earth; area and distance squeezed at the edge",
    epsg: "Rarely used for analysis",
    use: "Visual reference: compare the other maps with this one."
  }]
])








































































viewof projName = Inputs.select([...projections.keys()], {
  label: "Projection", value: "Mercator"
})
viewof layers = Inputs.checkbox(["Tissot circles", "Route: DC → London"], {
  value: ["Tissot circles", "Route: DC → London"]
})
















{
  const p = projections.get(projName);
  // Map uses the full page width (best when projected in class); the info
  // card sits underneath it
  const w = width;
  const h = Math.round(w * 0.58);

  // Build the projection and fit it to the world, Mercator's box, or North
  // America. clipExtent keeps conic/transverse maps from drawing off-screen.
  const projection = p.make()
    .fitExtent([[8, 8], [w - 8, h - 8]], fitTarget(p.view))
    .clipExtent([[0, 0], [w, h]]);
  const path = d3.geoPath(projection);

  const svg = d3.create("svg").attr("viewBox", [0, 0, w, h]).style("width", "100%");
  svg.append("path").attr("d", path({type: "Sphere"})).attr("fill", "#eef3f7");
  svg.append("path").attr("d", path(d3.geoGraticule10()))
    .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", "#cfd8e0").attr("stroke-width", 0.5);
  svg.append("path").attr("d", path(land)).attr("fill", "#c9c4b8");
  if (layers.includes("Tissot circles")) {
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(tissot))
      .attr("fill", "#d1495b").attr("fill-opacity", 0.55);
  }
  if (layers.includes("Route: DC → London")) {
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(route))
      .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", "#1f5f8b").attr("stroke-width", 2.5);
  }
  svg.append("path").attr("d", path({type: "Sphere"}))
    .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", "#9aa5ad");

  // Each fact is its own &lt;div&gt; so the card can flow into columns
  const card = html`&lt;dl class="proj-card"&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;&lt;dt&gt;Family&lt;/dt&gt;&lt;dd&gt;${p.family}&lt;/dd&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;&lt;dt&gt;Preserves&lt;/dt&gt;&lt;dd&gt;${p.keeps}&lt;/dd&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;&lt;dt&gt;Gives up&lt;/dt&gt;&lt;dd&gt;${p.gives}&lt;/dd&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;&lt;dt&gt;EPSG codes&lt;/dt&gt;&lt;dd&gt;${p.epsg}&lt;/dd&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;&lt;dt&gt;Use it for&lt;/dt&gt;&lt;dd&gt;${p.use}&lt;/dd&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/dl&gt;`;

  return html`&lt;div class="widget"&gt;${svg.node()}${card}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}






Things to try:

Mercator vs. Equal Earth: compare the circles near the poles. Which map keeps their size and which keeps their shape?
The DC → London route is the shortest path on the globe. On which projection is it a straight line? (Hint: one of them measures distance from DC.)
The two conic maps look almost the same over Maryland. Over that small area, the distortion differences are tiny. This is why a sensible local CRS matters much more than the “perfect” one.


How big is Greenland, really?
On Mercator, Greenland looks about as big as Africa. Drag it south and watch what happens on each map. Its true size never changes. The Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC, in blue) stays put for comparison.

viewof targetLat = Inputs.range([0, 73], {
  value: 73, step: 1, label: "Greenland's center latitude (° N)"
})







function moveTo(feature, newLat) {
  const [lon0, lat0] = d3.geoCentroid(feature);
  const toOrigin = d3.geoRotation([-lon0, -lat0]);   // centroid -&gt; (0, 0)
  const toTarget = d3.geoRotation([-lon0, -newLat]); // (lon0, newLat) -&gt; (0, 0)
  const move = pt =&gt; toTarget.invert(toOrigin(pt));
  const walk = c =&gt; typeof c[0] === "number" ? move(c) : c.map(walk);
  return {type: "Feature", geometry: {
    type: feature.geometry.type, coordinates: walk(feature.geometry.coordinates)
  }};
}

greenland = countries.find(d =&gt; d.properties.name === "Greenland")
drc = countries.find(d =&gt; d.properties.name === "Dem. Rep. Congo")
movedGreenland = moveTo(greenland, targetLat)

{
  const wide = width &gt; 700;
  const w = wide ? Math.floor((width - 40) / 2) : width;
  const h = Math.round(w * 0.75);

  // Draw one panel and report Greenland's apparent size relative to the DRC
  function panel(title, makeProjection, view) {
    const projection = makeProjection().fitExtent([[6, 6], [w - 6, h - 6]], fitTarget(view));
    const path = d3.geoPath(projection);
    const svg = d3.create("svg").attr("viewBox", [0, 0, w, h]).style("width", "100%");
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path({type: "Sphere"})).attr("fill", "#eef3f7");
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(land)).attr("fill", "#dcd8cf");
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(greenland))
      .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", "#d1495b").attr("stroke-dasharray", "3 3");
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(drc)).attr("fill", "#1f5f8b");
    svg.append("path").attr("d", path(movedGreenland)).attr("fill", "#d1495b");
    const looks = path.area(movedGreenland) / path.area(drc);
    return html`&lt;div&gt;
      &lt;div style="font-weight:600"&gt;${title}&lt;/div&gt;${svg.node()}
      &lt;div&gt;Greenland looks &lt;span class="readout"&gt;${looks.toFixed(1)}×&lt;/span&gt; the DRC&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;`;
  }

  // True ratio, measured on the globe
  const truth = d3.geoArea(greenland) / d3.geoArea(drc);

  return html`&lt;div class="widget"&gt;
    &lt;div style="display:${wide ? "grid" : "block"};grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem"&gt;
      ${panel("Mercator (conformal)", d3.geoMercator, "merc")}
      ${panel("Equal Earth (equal-area)", d3.geoEqualEarth, "world")}
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin:.75rem 0 0"&gt;On the globe, Greenland is
      &lt;b&gt;${truth.toFixed(2)}×&lt;/b&gt; the size of the DRC, wherever you put it.
      The dashed outline is where Greenland actually is.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}




































The same comparison in R, using the world dataset from the spData package:

# `world` (from spData) is in EPSG:4326
two &lt;- filter(world, name_long %in% c("Greenland", "Democratic Republic of the Congo"))

# The same two countries in two projected CRSs
two_merc &lt;- st_transform(two, 3857)   # Web Mercator
two_ee   &lt;- st_transform(two, 8857)   # Equal Earth

# Area on the globe (sf does this for lon/lat data), then on each flat map
area_globe &lt;- st_area(two)
area_merc  &lt;- st_area(two_merc)
area_ee    &lt;- st_area(two_ee)

# Convert from square meters to square kilometers
area_globe &lt;- set_units(area_globe, km^2)
area_merc  &lt;- set_units(area_merc, km^2)
area_ee    &lt;- set_units(area_ee, km^2)

data.frame(country = two$name_long, area_globe, area_merc, area_ee)

                           country     area_globe       area_merc        area_ee
1 Democratic Republic of the Congo 2333672 [km^2]  2358240 [km^2] 2323379 [km^2]
2                        Greenland 2189752 [km^2] 36285503 [km^2] 2206879 [km^2]








